The four isolated attack voices all used forward non-looping PCM at
pitch register 0xe9b. With the verified 3,579,545 Hz chip clock and
4096-minus-pitch period, the effective average rate rounds to 10027 Hz.
The active voice volume/pan pairs for Marge, Homer, Bart and Lisa were
47/2, 71/3, 47/5 and 39/6. Native attack playback now uses the observed
rate and channel gain ratios, accounting for the reference half-gain
stereo routes. Catalogue metadata remains at its nominal source rate.
This approximates each isolated voice through the shared mixer; it does
not reconstruct the chip's stepwise interpolation, music or full mix.

The native controller integration now refills vacant input slots after
removal, without reassigning the other stations. Synthetic input tests
exercise four moving stations, a detached controller's released direction,
a rejected stale button event and movement from its replacement. Repeated
native discovery is covered by the opt-in platform smoke. Physical
controller hot-plug and simultaneous hardware play remain unverified.

Matched original-machine diagonal, left-only, up-only and idle Marge
observations confirm two pixels per tick on each independent component.
The two repeated diagonal runs matched all 576 raw and RGB captures.
Isolated attacks for all four stations retained their opening facing
until recovery, even when Left was subsequently held. Marge accepted a
same-frame direction change on attack admission; a later direction
change did not turn the attack. Jump permitted a later turn and full
horizontal movement. Matched attack-direction observations show slow
horizontal drift and no vertical drift for Marge. The title now retains
attack facing after tick zero, models approximate quarter-pixel horizontal
movement, and resumes normal walking at recovery. Fractional rounding,
vertical drift for the other stations and combat hitboxes remain unverified.
Inline regression tests cover diagonal movement, all four stations' attack
facing and drift, recovery, and turning during jump. This supersedes the
previous provisional attack immobility rule.

Native display settings now select shared scaling and desktop fullscreen
through the local launch catalogue or command-line overrides. The fixed
street preview uses temporary aspect-fit fallback for adaptive mode;
real camera extension with additional imported scene content and responsive
HUD/touch placement is still required. Fill is unavailable with the fixed
touch panel to prevent clipping essential controls.

Local keyboard and controller-button profiles can override each of the
four stations independently. Regression tests route remapped inputs,
reject old bindings and verify the replacement controller receives the
vacant station's profile. A combined hidden title run passed with a
custom profile and integer scaling. Touch and analog remapping,
interactive settings and physical devices remain open.

The optional single touch panel is now selectable for any station, with
per-station button-position profiles. Inline tests verify moved glyphs,
hit regions and station routing. The combined hidden title run passed
with Bart selected, swapped attack/jump and left/right touch positions,
custom keyboard bindings and integer mode. Independent simultaneous
touch panels and hardware testing remain open.

Owner acceptance override: the visible prototype opens onto street art
without an obvious entry prompt; the owner observed a character only
after station Start. The existing coin/Start/continue evidence above
describes historical reference observations and temporary implementation,
not an approved mode. Replace the admission, HUD and paid continue
interactions with free play, shared modern menus and free local join.
Actual visible native play remains unverified by this development session.

The hidden display matrix passed five windowed modes and four fullscreen
touch modes. All four selected touch stations passed swapped directional
slots; catalogue-relative profile selection worked from another working
directory. Fill with the fixed touch panel and a symlink profile were
rejected. All fourteen successful matrix runs reported zero missed
samples across their respective 600-frame movement workloads.
Adaptive is still fixed-scene fallback, not extended content.

The modern free-play slice supersedes the temporary paid admission
described above. A shared deterministic local session owns the main
menu, pause menu, selection, reset and exit. The visible native menu
advertises the four configured keyboard Start keys. Enter starts
Marge immediately; the other stations can enter or join independently.
Controller Start and the selected touch panel join the assigned station;
there is no payment, coin control, credit balance or paid prompt. Vacant
stations advertise their join key. Knockout retains the provisional
1200-tick free rejoin opportunity; expiry of all stations returns to the
main menu and resets the encounter. Pause freezes the title world on
the input tick, including attack cues and countdowns. Regression tests
cover immediate join, held Start, expiry, pause at an attack cue, menu
wrap, reset and exit. Menu glyphs and station prompts share bounded,
persistent native text atlases, without per-frame texture uploads.

The native hidden smoke passed under Xvfb with the local actor, pose,
enemy and audio bundles, synthetic touch and a dummy audio device.
The run rendered all four stations, maneuvers, health and free rejoin
prompts; it reported one missed sample in the 600-frame movement
workload. This is not physical input, audio, GPU or desktop acceptance.
Interactive settings, independent controller character selection and
real adaptive scene extension remain open.

The shared free-play session now includes a display settings screen from
both the main and pause menus. Display cycling and desktop fullscreen
switching apply to the live renderer without rebuilding its atlases.
Menu framing temporarily uses aspect-fit for a cropped gameplay mode;
touch-enabled sessions skip Fill entirely. Native menus reuse compact
cached setting rows rather than rasterizing complete selection variants.
Engine tests verify all modes, the touch restriction and return to the
originating screen. The combined hidden title smoke additionally routed
keyboard actions into Settings, observed changed logical pixels, switched
to fullscreen and back, and returned to the main menu. It passed the
600-frame movement workload with zero missed samples. Real devices,
actual widescreen content and persistent display settings remain open.

Display preferences can now persist in an explicitly selected private
directory via the shared native atomic save envelope. A versioned engine
payload covers every display/window combination. Catalogue-relative paths,
CLI precedence, missing preferences and incompatible payloads are tested.
Stored Fill falls back to aspect-fit on touch launch; temporary menu
framing and smoke runs do not overwrite a saved value.

A visible X11 diagnostic under isolated Xvfb used the signed Fedora
xdotool package (extracted locally, not installed) to deliver native
keyboard events. Enter joined Marge, Right changed the rendered street
frame, Escape paused, and Down/Primary opened and edited Settings.
The integer/windowed preference was atomically saved. The first scripted
exit sequence selected the wrong menu entry and timed out; subsequent
launches exited normally through the menu. A repeat nonblack capture
showed the reopened settings panel pixel-identical to the saved panel;
the preference file hash remained unchanged. The hidden combined smoke
with an explicit stretch override also passed without modifying the
saved file, with one missed movement frame sample. This verifies the
visible native event and save path under Xvfb, not physical keyboard,
controller, touch, audio or GPU hardware. Captures remain private.

Character selection now follows the physical input seat independently of
the character. Left/Right cycles vacant characters before Start, swapping
only vacant assignments and preserving held-input ownership. Joined and
continuing characters cannot be taken by another seat. The main menu and
vacant-character join indicators show seat-specific selection and key
labels. Tests cover joining the selected character, movement and release
after selection, held selection without repeat, and skipping an active
character for late join. The combined native smoke verified selection
changes rendered pixels, Start joined the selected character, and the
four-player 600-frame workload missed zero frame-budget samples. The
synthetic controller and touch paths also joined selected characters.
Physical controller and touchscreen acceptance remain open.

Native attack audio follows session suspension: the device pauses and
the mixer stops advancing while paused or in Settings. Returning to the
main menu discards queued audio and active voices without allocating new
mixer storage. A shared mixer test verifies silence and slot reuse after
clearing. The native dummy-device smoke verifies pause, resume, and
discard on reset with previously submitted audio, in addition to the
existing held-attack checks. Actual audio hardware remains unverified.

The MAME importer can publish a continuous world background from two to
eight independently verified scenes. It requires equal positive
horizontal movement of both scrolling tile layers, unchanged vertical
offsets and exact RGBA overlap. Only explicitly excluded HUD rows and
verified opaque-black side margins are removed; differing overlap
rejects publication. Two Bart walking observations at device frames 2290
and 2311 (display frames 2291 and 2312) each reconstructed all 71,680
original pixels. Removing the top 28 HUD rows and 16 black pixels from
each side allowed a 42-pixel extension of the 288-pixel world interior.
The resulting 330-by-196 strip exactly matches the current Marge
background over all 56,448 base interior pixels. The local bundle is
available for adaptive rendering; no original assets are tracked.

The optional world manifest now extends the title's logical viewport
without changing the fixed-step simulation or stretching sprites.
Admission checks every base interior pixel and both black side margins,
and rejects transparent or oversized strips. The native renderer
reuses resident atlases while changing the logical target only when its
extent changes. Station indicators and health spread across the width;
menus and the single touch panel center, with inverse touch projection
and side-space rejection. Fixed modes and missing strips retain the
320-pixel base.

An isolated Xvfb run with SDL X11, software GL and dummy audio verified
the actual 362-by-224 and 362-by-304 logical readbacks: all 8,232
additional world pixels matched the imported strip. The two aspect-fit
runs stayed at 320 columns. All four combined 600-frame workloads
missed zero samples, including synthetic touch movement in adaptive
mode. A visible X11 run joined using Enter, resized from 960-by-672 to
1086-by-672 and matched all 126-by-588 scaled extension pixels; shrinking
restored the black right margin and the native menu exited normally.
Two earlier visible attempts timed out because the SDL video backend
was not explicitly X11. Current support is a bounded 42-column opening
street extension, not scrolling stage progression, complete widescreen
coverage or physical display/touch/controller acceptance.

A longer fresh-state network-isolated MAME observation held Bart's Right
input through device frame 2410. The earlier device frame 2290 and
screen frame 2291 reproduced byte-for-byte. At device frames 2290,
2310 and 2330 both moving layers advanced from 422 to 462 to 502,
then remained at 502 despite continued input. The three independently
verified scenes published an exactly overlapping 368-by-196 world
interior, extending the opening logical viewport to 400 columns.
Both 400-by-224 and 400-by-304 native readbacks matched all 15,680
additional world pixels, and both 600-frame workloads missed zero
samples. A visible X11 window resized to 1194-by-672 reproduced all
234-by-588 scaled extension pixels (398-by-224 logical viewport) and
exited through its menu. This covers the opening street at approximately
16:9 without changing simulation bounds or proving stage progression.
The observed scroll limit and later street geometry remain open.

The verified strip also enables bounded rightward movement in world
coordinates. The fixed-step cabinet advances a provisional original-width
camera by at most two pixels per tick, constrained by the leftmost active
station, living enemy and admitted strip extent. Late joins and free
rejoins spawn in the current camera area; pause freezes travel and a
world reset restores the starting origin. Presentation independently
projects the camera, actors and enemy health into the selected viewport;
a wider viewport shows more of the same gameplay state. Tests exercise
a waiting cooperative player, camera cap, late join, pause and reset.
The native X11 smoke reached the 80-column camera limit and checked
unobscured street rows against the source strip in both fixed-width and
adaptive presentations, with and without touch. Both 600-frame workloads
missed zero samples. The original camera trigger, collision rules, enemy
gates and progression beyond the imported street remain provisional.
A separate visible X11 keyboard run without the optional encounter held
Right in aspect-fit mode until the 80-column camera cap; all 864-by-84
captured unobscured street pixels matched the three-times-scaled strip,
and the game exited normally through the menu.

The concurrent storage migration agent reported an isolated Xvfb smoke
using upgraded version-two bundles, software GL and dummy audio. Across
67 bundle pairs, all 292 payload files (23,331,508 bytes) were identical.
The adaptive touch scene reached 400-by-304 logical pixels with 80
additional columns and the 80-column camera cap. Four-player movement
over 600 frames averaged 1.175294 ms, maximum 2.324734 ms, with no
16.666667 ms deadline misses. This is software-renderer regression
evidence, not physical GPU, device or complete gameplay acceptance.

An explicit native LAN-host catalogue now reserves authenticated remote
controller seats while preserving local shared-screen rendering. The
host owns menu and pause controls and uses the existing fixed-tick
simulation. No network opens by default. A separate controller client,
physical multi-device play and remote world presentation remain open.

A standalone GNU/Linux native controller can now send authenticated
keyboard or gamepad transitions to the shared-screen host without
original or canonical assets on the controller machine. Its window
provides input instructions rather than replicating the game world.
Loopback TLS tests verify ordered press and release delivery and
bounded backpressure; an opt-in software-renderer native smoke
verified the panel readback. Real multi-device acceptance remains open.

The controller and host also negotiate liveness through authenticated
heartbeat frames and an idle lease, without generating simulation input.
Loopback evidence verifies that a silent client loses its held direction
even when its local socket is still open, and its identity can rejoin.
Physical network interruption and real-device latency remain unverified.

Native menu hints now describe admitted launch capabilities. Settings
reports adaptive street extension only when a verified world strip is
available, otherwise it describes aspect-fit fallback. Hosted main menus
explain that a local station starts the world and remote stations join
while playing. A synthetic test checks both hint variants. Release-mode
Xvfb software-renderer and dummy-audio touch smoke runs with and without
the verified strip passed, with zero missed 16.666667 ms budget samples
across each 600-frame movement workload. The strip run verified the
400-column logical scene and 80-column camera travel. These runs do not
establish physical display or remote-device acceptance.

The optional native touch surface supports one to four independently
remappable local stations, with panels stacked in station order below
the verified gameplay extent. The existing single-panel default and
profile syntax remain compatible. Multi-panel rendering uses one
static atlas and sprite; the input router admits at most eight
contacts per configured panel in total. A reserved LAN seat cannot
be assigned a touch panel. Physical touchscreen testing remains open.

Three optimized native smoke runs used verified local version-two art,
Xvfb software OpenGL and dummy audio: four seats at adaptive 400x544,
seats two and four at aspect-fit 320x384, and the legacy seat-three
selection at 320x304. All selected seats joined through their panels,
held two movement contacts at once, moved independently and stopped
on release. The four-seat profile swapped seat two's horizontal slots;
the two-seat profile swapped seat four's action slots. Each run also
verified the 80-column street camera, 600 movement submissions and
51 combat submissions without missing the 60 Hz budget. These are
synthetic contacts through the native projection, not physical devices.

The reference now distinguishes short and held jumps: Marge's 3-, 20-
and 60-tick button holds produced 31, 51 and 58 displayed airborne
ticks. Fresh isolated 60-tick holds for Bart and Lisa also produced
58-tick sequences; Homer's late landing was interrupted by an enemy.
The title implements bounded quarter-pixel lift with stronger upward
deceleration after release and ordinary descent. A short tap lasts
31 ticks, a 20-tick hold 49 ticks, and an uninterrupted hold 58 ticks.
Release is latched for the current flight; another press cannot renew
lift. Each station retains independent ground movement and attack
admission. The existing short-jump pose catalogue is reused and its
final pose is held beyond its range. This is a timing and height
approximation, not reconstructed original physics or new long-jump
art. Intermediate original timings, pivots, collisions and full
enemy interaction remain unverified.

Fresh normal workspace validation and native offscreen touch smoke
with locally verified poses, encounter and audio passed. The native
smoke now also checks prolonged lift after the short-tap landing
time, changed frame pixels, unchanged ground anchor and landing.
The separate opt-in workspace controller smoke exposed concurrent
SDL initialization between its two test threads; that issue was
reported to the network-contract owner for independent correction.

Four fresh original held-jump observations captured the distinct
middle pose around the eleventh displayed tick. The existing exact
scene importer accepted all four aligned scene pairs and published
the corresponding isolated actor groups under the ignored local
bundle directory. The optional catalogue admits four new
`jump-lift 0` entries together (one per character) within its
unchanged 64-pose bound. Opening and middle pose selection uses the
simulated ascent; short taps and legacy catalogues retain their
existing selection. The four new actor crops are locally verified,
but phase transitions and actor pivots remain approximations.

The combined 63-entry local catalogue passed native offscreen
touch-and-pose smoke with the held-jump readback check. The regression
tests also reject partial or out-of-range lift groups and compare
legacy, short-tap, opening, raised and descent selection.

Five additional isolated Marge captures of 5-, 10-, 15-, 25- and
30-tick holds produced respectively 35, 40, 46, 54 and 58 displayed
airborne ticks. The 10-tick hold skipped the middle pose; the 15-
and 25-tick holds selected it and then returned to the descent pose
before landing. Presentation now ends the middle pose on simulated
release or tick 31, whichever occurs first. Exact input-to-pose
latency and the intermediate hold trajectory remain approximate.

Native control profiles now reuse the bounded atomic save envelope in the
dedicated settings directory, independently of display settings. Explicit
profile selection persists only after successful native startup. Saved
bindings override the catalogue, and an explicit reset restores default
bindings on later launches. Fresh workspace validation exercises relaunch
precedence, malformed profiles, independent display preferences, and
read-only smoke selection. Interactive remapping and physical-device
acceptance remain open.

The optimized native profile diagnostic at 0716299 confirmed cabinet
keyboard remapping, persistence through relaunch, and restoration of the
default binding after reset. The same test exercised an authenticated
standalone controller with a separate settings directory. It used
synthetic native X11 keyboard events and verified local art; physical
controller and touchscreen acceptance remain open.

Further inspection of the seven isolated Marge captures compared each
actor's lower sprite-cell edge with its stationary shadow anchor.
The observed opening, middle and descent groups share the same
quarter-pixel trajectory when the first release transition occurs
one displayed tick after the corresponding held duration. Landing
occurs when the remaining elevation rounds down to zero pixels.
The revised fixed-step model matches every sampled displayed height
for 5- and 15-tick holds and the durations and peaks for all seven
holds (5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30 and 60 ticks). The peak heights range
from 60 to 108 logical pixels. The 20-tick hold now lasts 51 instead
of 49 ticks. Pose selection retains the appropriate ascent pose
until that same delayed transition. A release followed by another
press within one input batch cannot restore lift.

Fresh workspace validation passed, including full height sequences,
peak and duration checks, input-batch release and native pose selection.
A native offscreen smoke with local verified 63-pose art, touch controls,
encounter and dummy audio also passed: all 600 movement frames and
51 combat submissions met the 16.667 ms budget. These measurements
establish selected stationary Marge behavior and not exact opaque-pixel
pivots, moving or interrupted jumps, or complete game parity.

Isolated Marge jump-attack captures at five, fifteen and thirty
elapsed jump ticks yielded respectively 33, 21 and 24 attack
frames and 66, 84 and 96 pixels of forward travel. Additional
captures bracketed the transition between 61 and 66 pixels of
initial elevation; attacking late in a short jump still selected
the slower rate. The fixed-step model selects a 64-pixel threshold
and reproduces the three complete measured height and distance
traces with sixteenth-pixel arithmetic. Captures of Homer, Bart and
Lisa also showed the four-pixel forward rate from the higher jump.

Primary during an airborne jump now initiates an aerial attack;
existing ground attacks remain uninterrupted by jump. The preview
temporarily selects ground-attack art for the new maneuver. A
single provisional encounter hit per flight uses the existing
horizontal and depth ranges with a 64-pixel elevation limit.
Direction locking, exact threshold, enemy reactions, hitboxes and
other characters' complete trajectories are not established as
original parity. Simultaneous buttons produced a separate stationary
airborne attack in the original; that distinct maneuver remains open.

Fresh workspace validation passed, including the complete three
height traces, threshold samples, landing, independent station
state, forward movement, and one-hit encounter behavior.

Four additional isolated scene captures supplied thirteen distinct
aerial attack poses: five for Marge, two each for Homer and Bart,
and four for Lisa. All thirteen aligned scene comparisons passed
exactly (71,680 pixels each) before local canonical publication.
The optional air-attack catalogue requires all four tick-zero
records and bounds offsets below 60; the 16 KiB byte cap remains,
with the independent entry limit expanded from 64 to 96.
Older catalogues continue to select provisional ground-attack
art for the aerial maneuver. Crops retain approximate pivots.

Both the combined 76-pose catalogue and the earlier 63-pose
catalogue passed native offscreen touch-and-combat smoke, including
the new airborne attack readback and measured 84-pixel landing
displacement. The new catalogue's 600 movement and 51 combat
submissions missed no 16.667 ms budget. The measured results are
host submission timings, not physical presentation latency.
Fresh workspace validation passed, including incomplete catalogue
rejection and legacy versus aerial pose selection. Proprietary
observations and canonical assets remain in ignored local storage.

Four isolated simultaneous-button observations established a
30-tick stationary attack with no repetition during a sustained
60-tick hold. Homer's measured lower-cell height trace matches
a symmetric 45-pixel peak. The shared model requires overlapping
press edges in one simulation tick, independent of input order;
nonoverlapping taps retain their isolated actions. It uses a
provisional single encounter hit and locks direction and movement.
The precise timing tolerance, collision rules, sound and individual
actor pivots are not established as original parity.

Twelve representative special poses (three per character) passed
exact aligned full-scene comparisons before local publication.
Bart's opening sample uses the first visible actor body, one frame
after the effect-only opening. The optional catalogue requires
all four tick-zero records, bounds offsets below 30, and uses
aerial or ground-attack art as fallback. Samples do not establish
complete animation parity; original media remains private.

Fresh validation and native offscreen touch-and-combat smoke
passed with the combined 88-pose catalogue and the previous
76-pose catalogue. The smoke checks simultaneous key edges,
visible special presentation, and landing at the original
position. Both runs missed no 16.667 ms submission budget
across 600 movement and 51 combat frames.

Isolated original Marge probes compared three-tick button pulses and
both three- and sixty-tick jump holds. Jump-first attack offsets one,
two and three reproduced all 96 sampled jump-only object-DMA frames;
offsets four and five (short hold) changed the output. Attack-first
one- and two-tick overlaps reproduced all 96 attack-only frames, not
the simultaneous special. Separate sixty-tick jump controls for Homer,
Bart and Lisa matched all 101 object-DMA frames at offset three and
diverged at offset four. The shared fixed-step model now rejects early
aerial attack edges through the first three input offsets without
buffering a held attack. A new edge at offset four is accepted for each
station. These observations bound initial input timing, not all airborne
attack conditions or full original gameplay parity.

A sixty-tick primary hold beginning at jump input offset three also
matched all 96 sampled long-jump-only object-DMA frames for Marge.
Seven additional three-tick-jump probes established that offset 28
through 31 still selects the forward aerial attack with a shadow;
offsets 32 through 34 select a ground attack after landing. The
existing simulation already distinguishes offsets 31 and 32, and
fixed-step regressions now preserve that boundary. The late samples
establish action selection, not the complete late attack trajectory.

The native smoke now routes a synthetic primary pulse on the first
post-takeoff tick, checks that jump and position remain intact, and
then verifies the existing later aerial attack, rendered pose and
84-pixel landing displacement. The optimized 3,224,528-byte build
passed this smoke with the verified 88-pose catalogue, adaptive
400x304 scene, touch controls and offscreen NVIDIA OpenGL ES.
The 600 movement and 51 combat submissions missed no 16.667 ms
host-work budget; maximum measured movement work was 3.285 ms.
Lifetime peak RSS was 90,300,416 bytes. This remains synthetic
input and offscreen submission evidence, not physical device or
presentation latency acceptance.

Repeated ground-attack and jump edges after an isolated ground attack
were compared with each character's original attack-only object-DMA
capture. Each of the eight offset-19 comparisons matched all 101
sampled frames; each offset-20 comparison diverged. Marge's attack
offsets 17 and 18 and jump offset 17 also matched all 101 frames.
The preview now retains a station-local 20-tick input recovery
interval without extending the observed 17- or 18-tick attack poses.
Early edges are discarded rather than buffered. Active simulation
ticks advance the recovery; pause does not. Movement and facing
remain governed by the existing pose timeline. Damage cancels the
provisional attack and its recovery. The original directional and
damage-interruption recovery behavior still needs separate evidence.

Regression coverage distinguishes offsets 17 through 20 for both
buttons and all four characters, and checks station independence,
held-edge rejection and pause. The encounter smoke exercises three
distinct strikes at the new cadence, including the idle recovery
frames, and the pose smoke rejects an additional primary edge at
offset 19 before the next distinct maneuver.

Fresh validation passed, including Android ARM64 and WASM core checks,
in run 1795617760177114640-2916614 (1,031 test executions). The release
build passed adaptive 400x304 offscreen OpenGL ES smoke with the
verified 88-pose catalogue and touch enabled. It submitted 600
